#Introduction
Platforms in the gaming industry have evolved drastically over many decades where things like convenience and accessibility to games have improved and changed the way people use gaming technology. However the evolution of gaming has created ethical challenges with consumer rights and accessibility.

#Age Restricted Content
In the days of arcade gaming, anyone could walk in and play whatever video game the place had to offer, as graphics got better there came more violent games such as mortal kombat, this created concern over kids being able to play these and view graphic content without any kind of restriction. With home gaming copies of violent games there was concern over the same issue, which is what led to PEGI becoming a thing in europe where age ratings are displayed on a game so stores can check a customers age before selling (PEGI, n.d.). Digital purchases face this issue too with kids being able to fake their age and gain access to parent accounts to buy age restricted content, to prevent this stronger parental controls and purchase approvals using things like digital id verification can help reduce problems like this.



#Transactions
Spending money on games is another ethical concern to look at, in the arcade era kids would spend money on games played and some took advantage of things like charging for extra lives to continue playing(Lambie, 2018), this can raise concern about children with little awareness around spending. This issue evolved with gaming especially with online games with microtransactions for digital goods and loot boxes that are similar to gambling (Zendle and Cairns, 2018). There was a case in 2023 where the FTC made Epic games refund $245 million worth of transactions because of unwanted purchases (Federal Trade Commission, 2023), some of which were from children using their parents credit card saved on the console. Things like this can be prevented with more protection around transactions using payment approvals and parental controls.


#Game ownership / access to patches and fixes
There is concern around if a game is released with bugs that need fixing and can only be fixed through online updates that might not be available to someone who purchased a physical copy of the game and has no access to wifi. Some modern game discs or cartridges only work as keys for the user to download the game (Nintendo, 2025) or need additional files which links into the no wifi issue. Companies need to make it clear to consumers that some games or parts of the game will not function properly without internet and ensuring games are stable from release can help reduce these issues.

The european commission has noticed that if a game company decides to shut their servers down it can cause parts of or the entire game to become unplayable. This raises more questions to consumers about whether or not they have full ownership of their product (European Commission, 2026). A game server staying online forever is unrealistic due to running costs but companies could facilitate offline play or community ran servers for certain games to help with this.

Cloud gaming services have became more widely used with better internet allowing people to stream gamed that would require expensive and powerful hardware to play natively. Companies with access to large servers such as Amazon and Microsoft run services like these but the reliability of them is dependent on both the users wifi and the stability of the server. There is a concern over the shutdown of these services, a good example is Google Stadia that ran from 2019 to 2023 and when it stopped all of its players lost access to games they had bought and played through its service, to protect customers they refunded what purchases they could (Google, 2023), but consumers were left without access to progress they made on some games. They could have improved on this situation by giving players a way to access or transfer their save files to another service.



#Hardware Failures
In the arcade era machines would occasionally go out of order for hardware or software related reasons. If this happened the arcade would be responsible for repairing the machines. The move to home gaming hardware failures became more of a concern to the consumer who has to deal with warranties and repairs.

An example of a widespread hardware failure on home consoles is when the xbox 360 first released it had a very common fatal hardware failure on many consoles called the red ring of death. This left a mark on microsofts reputation as it left lots of people who had spent lots of money with a bricked console. This cost Microsoft an estimated 1.1 billion dollars to extend affected consoles warranties for 3 years and replace them (Microsoft, 2007).

This affected many people who owned a 360 in its early days as they had to go through the process of replacing and repairing, broken consoles possibly being disposed of is also an environmental concern and shows how important it is for companies manufactoring gaming consoles to be responsible and aware of hardware issues with their products. Good measures to prevent or reduce damage in cases like this are better longevity and reliability tests in factories to ensure their product is high quality before reaching consumers and making repair services more accessible to consumers with broken hardware.





